.TH GOCP 1 "2026-11-16" "gosh" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
gocp \- copy files over a gosh session, carrying on where a copy left off
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gocp
[\fIoptions\fR] \fIsource\fR... \fIdestination\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B gocp
copies files to or from another machine, as \fBscp\fR(1) does, through a \fBgosh\fR(1) session: the
copy survives losing the network, changing networks, and sleep. Exactly one end is remote:
[\fIuser\fB@\fR]\fIhost\fB:\fIpath\fR.
.PP
A copy cut off and run again sends only what had not arrived, after checking that what had
arrived is the start of the same file. A file appears under its own name only when the whole of it
has arrived and its checksum agrees. A file already at the destination with the same size and
modification time is not sent. A receiving end writes only inside its destination, whatever names
the sending end gives it.
.PP
\fBNot independently reviewed.\fR See https://wmacevoy.github.io/gosh/USING.html.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-r
Directories, and everything in them.
.TP
.B \-\-auto
When the copy is done, stay, and copy again whatever changes at the source, until interrupted.
One way only.
.TP
.B \-\-delete
Also remove from the destination what is no longer at the source, within the directories copied.
.TP
\fB\-\-interval\fR=\fIseconds\fR
How often \fB\-\-auto\fR looks for changes (default: 2).
.TP
.B \-q
Say only what goes wrong.
.PP
The options of \fBgosh\fR(1) that concern the connection (\fB\-\-ssh\fR, \fB\-\-relay\fR, \fB\-p\fR,
\fB\-\-server\fR, ...) work here.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
gocp report.pdf server:docs/          to the server
gocp \-r server:build/out .            from it, with what is inside
gocp \-\-auto \-r server:build/out .     and then keep ./out up to date
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when everything was copied; 1 when anything was not (it says which), or the session ended first.
Run it again to carry on.
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBgosh\fR(1), \fBscp\fR(1), \fBrsync\fR(1). https://wmacevoy.github.io/gosh/
